WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:17.126 --> 00:00:19.333
Culture just exists,
whether you want it or not.

00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:24.287
If you don't manage it properly,
you might create negative and

00:00:24.287 --> 00:00:28.877
counterproductive cultures
that don't really emphasize

00:00:28.877 --> 00:00:33.108
the things that are going
to serve your organization.

00:00:33.108 --> 00:00:35.329
[MUSIC]

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:39.769
Culture was readily dismissed up
until the late 1970s as something

00:00:39.769 --> 00:00:42.516
managers don't really need to care about.

00:00:42.516 --> 00:00:46.072
But as organizations and
work becomes more complex, and

00:00:46.072 --> 00:00:50.963
people no longer work on an assembly line
where you can basically procedurally

00:00:50.963 --> 00:00:55.482
tell them what to do and it doesn't
really matter what they think because

00:00:55.482 --> 00:00:58.766
the assembly line solves
all the dilemmas for them.

00:00:58.766 --> 00:01:02.066
As robots are replacing this,
and people are put more and

00:01:02.066 --> 00:01:05.177
more in positions where they
need to make decisions.

00:01:05.177 --> 00:01:09.469
There's no way we can exhaustively kind of
describe all the situations that employees

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:12.835
are going to deal with, and
that's where culture comes into play.

00:01:12.835 --> 00:01:17.355
It's when there are these implicit
understandings about what is important and

00:01:17.355 --> 00:01:20.932
what isn't important, and
what we're trying to maximize and

00:01:20.932 --> 00:01:24.930
what we're not trying to maximize,
and who we are and who we aren't.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.653
And there's also this kind of
moral code that holds us together.

00:01:28.653 --> 00:01:33.901
Then you would expect that, on average,
employees would do the right thing

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:38.740
even if you haven't specified precisely
in the recipe of how to do it.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:43.294
So that's why culture is
becoming increasingly

00:01:43.294 --> 00:01:46.116
more important in organizations, and

00:01:46.116 --> 00:01:51.940
is increasingly recognized by leaders as
something that they need to invest in.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.930
[MUSIC]

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:58.253
We basically find that people
follow one of three trajectories.

00:01:58.253 --> 00:02:02.265
Either they show a gradual increase
in their level of cultural fit.

00:02:02.265 --> 00:02:06.674
So basically they're slowly learning
the code and behaving appropriately.

00:02:06.674 --> 00:02:09.827
These are people who stay in
the organization and succeed.

00:02:09.827 --> 00:02:14.190
We see people who never really show an
increase in their level of cultural fit,

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.305
relative to what they were immediately
after joining the organization.

00:02:18.305 --> 00:02:21.722
So that indicates to us that
they're either incapable or

00:02:21.722 --> 00:02:23.723
unwilling to adapt culturally.

00:02:23.723 --> 00:02:26.813
And those are the people at
extreme risk of being fired.

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:31.442
So basically these people,
if they survive a year, and

00:02:31.442 --> 00:02:37.263
they rarely do, they have a 50%
chance of being fired by that time.

00:02:37.263 --> 00:02:40.973
And finally, we find, I think, the most
interesting group of people are those who

00:02:40.973 --> 00:02:42.722
demonstrate this inverted U-shape.

00:02:42.722 --> 00:02:45.337
These people who
are capable of adapting but

00:02:45.337 --> 00:02:49.446
at some stage start showing a decline
in their level of cultural fit.

00:02:49.446 --> 00:02:53.354
And that's a strong indicator of
people's intention to leave voluntarily.

00:02:53.354 --> 00:02:58.128
So really we see these different
signatures in terms of people's

00:02:58.128 --> 00:03:00.561
enculturation trajectories and

00:03:00.561 --> 00:03:04.749
how those relate to their
outcomes in the organization.

00:03:04.749 --> 00:03:06.055
[MUSIC]

00:03:06.055 --> 00:03:13.225
So imagine a group of software engineers
that all sit together in the same room and

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:17.771
play ping pong in after hours and
know one another.

00:03:17.771 --> 00:03:25.266
Those type of groups can really
tolerate the nonconformist weirdo.

00:03:25.266 --> 00:03:28.393
And that might actually
be a very good thing for

00:03:28.393 --> 00:03:32.562
the organization because that weirdo,
so to speak, right,

00:03:32.562 --> 00:03:37.533
this person who is not behaving in
the appropriate way it's probably also

00:03:37.533 --> 00:03:42.103
the reflection of the fact that that
person is bringing new ideas and

00:03:42.103 --> 00:03:46.714
new sensitivities and has different
ways of thinking of problems.

00:03:46.714 --> 00:03:51.701
And that might be really important for
the organization's success,

00:03:51.701 --> 00:03:55.929
especially if this organization
needs some innovation and

00:03:55.929 --> 00:04:00.765
new thoughts in order to be successful
with its product offerings.

00:04:00.765 --> 00:04:06.990
So we find that there's this
tradeoff between your ability,

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:11.186
or tendency to behave in a compliant way.

00:04:11.186 --> 00:04:13.744
But also this security, or lack thereof,

00:04:13.744 --> 00:04:18.451
that you gain from the extent to which
you're part of a tight knit group or not.

00:04:18.451 --> 00:04:25.085
And really being in the right
kind of sweet spot leads to

00:04:25.085 --> 00:04:30.142
the greatest success in organizations.

00:04:30.142 --> 00:04:31.663
[MUSIC]

00:04:31.663 --> 00:04:34.584
On average,
having a strong culture is a good thing.

00:04:34.584 --> 00:04:38.542
If everybody is aligned on
the same important values, and

00:04:38.542 --> 00:04:43.470
if we would expect therefore that faced
with the same kind of situational

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:47.913
dilemmas, everybody would solve
them in roughly the same way and

00:04:47.913 --> 00:04:51.914
in a way that's conducive to
the organization strategy.

00:04:51.914 --> 00:04:56.419
Then yes, a strong culture is going to
be beneficial for the organization.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:04:59.590
But at the same time,
you don't want echo chambers,

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:03.551
especially if you're an organisation
that competes on novelty.

00:05:03.551 --> 00:05:08.370
If you're a Google, or an aspiring Google,
or if you're an Amazon, or

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:13.979
if you're even innovating in retail and
if you're innovating in transportation,

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:17.781
you don't want your organization
to be an echo chamber.

00:05:17.781 --> 00:05:23.649
You want to empower your employees
to also take risks sometimes and

00:05:23.649 --> 00:05:25.967
think outside of the box.

00:05:25.967 --> 00:05:27.749
[MUSIC]

00:05:27.749 --> 00:05:30.820
Some organizations are architected in

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:35.476
a way where actually people
aren't part of strong groups.

00:05:35.476 --> 00:05:40.288
They kind of crisscross
different functional roles

00:05:40.288 --> 00:05:43.906
in teams in a matrix kind of structure.

00:05:43.906 --> 00:05:48.039
So in those organizations people
would probably put a strong

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:51.039
emphasis on being culturally compliant and

00:05:51.039 --> 00:05:56.807
there will be a strong rewards to strong
cultures that everybody is aligned around.

00:05:56.807 --> 00:06:01.505
However, you can image other types
of organizations that are built of

00:06:01.505 --> 00:06:06.123
strongly connected groups,
where inside the group people have very

00:06:06.123 --> 00:06:11.081
strong familiarity with one another and
very intimate with one another.

00:06:11.081 --> 00:06:13.466
In those groups you can afford, or

00:06:13.466 --> 00:06:18.821
those individuals can afford to behave
in a culturally non-compliant way.

00:06:18.821 --> 00:06:21.475
And it might be the case
that those groups,

00:06:21.475 --> 00:06:25.901
if they have a good mix of people
who are compliant and non-compliant,

00:06:25.901 --> 00:06:30.654
are the ones who might come up with
the most brilliant and innovative ideas.

00:06:30.654 --> 00:06:35.514
So it's not that there's a kind
of one size fit all recipe

00:06:35.514 --> 00:06:40.375
that facilitates how culture
kind of translates into good

00:06:40.375 --> 00:06:45.553
operational results from
an organizational point of view.

00:06:45.553 --> 00:06:51.356
Different organizations,
different organizational architectures,

00:06:51.356 --> 00:06:56.501
and different strategic imperatives,
prescribe different and

00:06:56.501 --> 00:07:02.784
intricate relationships between structure
and culture in the organization.

00:07:02.784 --> 00:07:03.640
[MUSIC]

